## Capturing Values with @ Bindings

The `@` (at) pattern binds a value to a name while also matching a subpattern. It lets you access the whole value and its destructured parts at the same time.

### Basic @ Pattern

```xiom
pub type Shape = enum {
  Circle(radius: Float64),
  Rect(w: Float64, h: Float64),
}

fn describe(s: &Shape) -> Str {
  match s {
    Circle(radius) if radius > 100.0 => {
      // Need radius in the message but also used in guard
      return "Large circle r=" + radius;
    }
    _ => { return "Other shape"; }
  }
}
```

### Binding Example with Range Check

```xiom
pub type Reading = enum {
  Temperature(value: Float64),
  Error,
}

fn classify(r: &Reading) -> Str {
  match r {
    Temperature(value) if value < 0.0 => { return "Freezing"; }
    Temperature(value) if value < 20.0 => { return "Cold"; }
    Temperature(value) if value < 30.0 => { return "Warm"; }
    Temperature(value) => { return "Hot"; }
    Error => { return "Error"; }
  }
}
```

### Binding in Variable Patterns

You can bind and match in let statements:

```xiom
pub type Pair = enum {
  Numbers(a: Int, b: Int),
}

let p = Pair.Numbers(10, 20);
match p {
  Numbers(a, b) => {
    // use both a and b
    io.println("a=" + a + ", b=" + b);
  }
}
```

### When to Use Bindings

- When you need both a captured value AND to match against it
- When destructuring a variant with multiple fields you need individually
- When nesting match patterns deep within data structures

